1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:05,206
Battery packs are everywhere: scooters, robots, and small storage systems.

2
00:00:05,206 --> 00:00:07,026
But the states that matter are hidden.

3
00:00:07,026 --> 00:00:14,987
The operator sees voltage, current, and surface temperature, not the state of charge, the internal resistance, or the core temperature.

4
00:00:14,987 --> 00:00:17,475
Failures are often handled after they happen.

5
00:00:17,475 --> 00:00:23,665
This project builds a digital twin for a 48 volt, thirteen cell pack, about ten amp hours.

6
00:00:23,665 --> 00:00:33,058
The user is a fleet or storage operator who needs three answers: how much runtime is left, is the pack getting less healthy, and will the planned load overheat it?

7
00:00:33,058 --> 00:00:40,885
The twin estimates hidden states from measurements, predicts remaining runtime, explains anomalies, and supports what-if decisions.

8
00:00:40,885 --> 00:00:49,381
We built all five components: acquisition, MQTT communication, data management, a hybrid model, and a user service.

9
00:00:49,381 --> 00:00:53,300
Every claim is backed by an executable check or a measured number.

10
00:00:54,402 --> 00:00:57,034
So the project is built around hidden states.

11
00:00:57,034 --> 00:01:01,585
On the left are the measurements: voltage, current, and surface temperature.

12
00:01:01,585 --> 00:01:09,740
In the middle are the three questions that operators actually ask: how much runtime is left, is the pack degrading, and will a planned load overheat it?

13
00:01:09,740 --> 00:01:11,742
On the right is what the twin adds.

14
00:01:11,742 --> 00:01:20,383
An extended Kalman filter fuses measurements; residuals detect and explain faults; and forward simulation answers what-if scenarios.

15
00:01:20,383 --> 00:01:26,111
This is the value of a digital twin: it turns raw signals into an actionable estimate.

16
00:01:27,002 --> 00:01:29,379
The architecture has five moving parts.

17
00:01:29,379 --> 00:01:32,837
On the left, the plant simulator represents the real world.

18
00:01:32,837 --> 00:01:41,660
It uses a two-RC electrical model, a two-node thermal model, temperature-dependent resistance, aging drift, noise, and packet loss.

19
00:01:41,660 --> 00:01:45,289
It publishes telemetry at one hertz over MQTT.

20
00:01:45,289 --> 00:01:48,165
The same broker feeds the ingestor and the twin.

21
00:01:48,165 --> 00:01:51,600
The ingestor validates data and writes SQLite.

22
00:01:51,600 --> 00:02:00,168
The twin runs a deliberately different model: one RC, one thermal node, an extended Kalman filter, and online resistance tracking.

23
00:02:00,168 --> 00:02:02,376
It publishes state and alarms.

24
00:02:02,376 --> 00:02:07,000
The dashboard consumes live MQTT and historical SQLite data.

25
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:09,500
The critical design choice is separation.

26
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:14,342
Plant parameters are private, and truth is written only to a local CSV.

27
00:02:14,342 --> 00:02:16,515
The twin never subscribes to truth.

28
00:02:16,515 --> 00:02:19,342
That avoids validating a model against itself.

29
00:02:20,218 --> 00:02:32,655
The acquisition table has five signals: pack voltage and current at one hertz, surface temperature at one hertz, ambient temperature at zero point one hertz, and a sequence plus source timestamp on every message.

30
00:02:32,655 --> 00:02:34,670
Current is positive on discharge.

31
00:02:34,670 --> 00:02:41,041
The simulator adds voltage, current, and temperature noise, quantization, and a small packet loss rate.

32
00:02:41,041 --> 00:02:47,425
It also supports a simulation speed multiplier, so a two-hour discharge can be demonstrated in six minutes.

33
00:02:47,425 --> 00:02:51,624
Source time is simulation time; ingest time is wall-clock time.

34
00:02:51,624 --> 00:03:02,995
The assumptions are explicit: cells are balanced, capacity is constant within a run, the OCV curve comes from literature, convection is fixed, and hysteresis is ignored.

35
00:03:02,995 --> 00:03:07,473
The plant uses private parameters; the twin only receives telemetry.

36
00:03:08,370 --> 00:03:10,929
Communication uses MQTT only.

37
00:03:10,929 --> 00:03:21,135
There are separate topics for telemetry, metadata, status, twin state, twin alarms, load commands, fault injection, and what-if requests and responses.

38
00:03:21,135 --> 00:03:24,133
Telemetry and twin outputs use QoS one.

39
00:03:24,133 --> 00:03:31,086
Meta and status are retained, so a dashboard that connects late immediately knows the units and the asset state.

40
00:03:31,086 --> 00:03:37,737
Status also configures a last will: if the simulator dies, the broker publishes offline, retained.

41
00:03:37,737 --> 00:03:43,853
The ingestor uses a persistent session, so a normal disconnect does not drop QoS one messages.

42
00:03:43,853 --> 00:03:45,941
Commands are logged to SQLite.

43
00:03:45,941 --> 00:03:47,640
Field names carry units.

44
00:03:47,640 --> 00:03:52,239
In the demo you will see actual topic traffic and the resulting dashboard updates.

45
00:03:53,114 --> 00:03:59,459
Data management starts with a raw message table that keeps every JSON payload, topic, and arrival time.

46
00:03:59,459 --> 00:04:08,003
Then a narrow clean table stores asset, variable, value, unit, source time, ingest time, sequence, and quality.

47
00:04:08,003 --> 00:04:12,517
Every message is range checked; impossible values are marked invalid.

48
00:04:12,517 --> 00:04:15,309
A uniqueness constraint removes duplicates.

49
00:04:15,309 --> 00:04:19,083
Sequence gaps are recorded as gaps, not filled with zero.

50
00:04:19,083 --> 00:04:24,047
For offline analysis, a one-hertz alignment function keeps missing values as NaN.

51
00:04:24,047 --> 00:04:27,396
The database uses WAL and batched commits.

52
00:04:27,396 --> 00:04:32,080
Queries and CSV export are available from the dashboard and a small CLI.

53
00:04:32,080 --> 00:04:37,578
This separation keeps the twin auditable: raw data is never overwritten by cleaning.

54
00:04:38,458 --> 00:04:40,908
The plant and twin intentionally differ.

55
00:04:40,908 --> 00:04:42,946
The plant is a two-RC model.

56
00:04:42,946 --> 00:04:50,446
Its resistance follows an Arrhenius temperature law, and it has two thermal nodes, core and surface, plus aging drift.

57
00:04:50,446 --> 00:04:54,039
The twin is a one-RC model with a single thermal node.

58
00:04:54,039 --> 00:05:01,005
Its voltage is OCV of state of charge, minus current times R zero, minus the RC polarization.

59
00:05:01,005 --> 00:05:06,963
State of charge integrates current; the thermal node balances Joule heating against convection.

60
00:05:06,963 --> 00:05:09,961
The twin does not trust the initial state of charge.

61
00:05:09,961 --> 00:05:13,565
It starts at sixty percent while the plant starts at ninety-five.

62
00:05:13,565 --> 00:05:19,111
An extended Kalman filter estimates SoC, RC voltage, and surface temperature.

63
00:05:19,111 --> 00:05:27,279
A recursive least-squares tracker updates resistance, and a short-window regression separates a resistance change from a voltage-sensor offset.

64
00:05:27,279 --> 00:05:31,672
State of health is nominal resistance divided by estimated resistance.

65
00:05:31,672 --> 00:05:39,415
Offline, parameters are identified from HPPC data using least squares on measured voltage, current, and temperature only.

66
00:05:39,415 --> 00:05:42,473
The fit is eight point eight millivolts RMS.

67
00:05:43,338 --> 00:05:44,877
Now the quantitative part.

68
00:05:44,877 --> 00:05:50,423
First, open-loop voltage accuracy on a drive cycle that was not used for identification.

69
00:05:50,423 --> 00:05:56,504
The one-RC twin has thirteen millivolts RMS error and forty-two millivolts maximum error.

70
00:05:56,504 --> 00:06:02,147
A no-relaxation baseline is two hundred eighty-six millivolts, so the RC branch matters.

71
00:06:02,147 --> 00:06:03,991
Second, state of charge.

72
00:06:03,991 --> 00:06:10,521
Starting deliberately wrong by thirty-five points, the filter converges within two percent in six seconds.

73
00:06:10,521 --> 00:06:14,550
After convergence, RMSE is zero point one one percent.

74
00:06:14,550 --> 00:06:19,574
A pure coulomb counter with the same wrong initial value stays at thirty-five percent error.

75
00:06:19,574 --> 00:06:21,188
Third, endurance.

76
00:06:21,188 --> 00:06:28,919
At ten checkpoints on a two-hour drive cycle, the twin predicts time to cutoff with four point two percent mean absolute percentage error.

77
00:06:28,919 --> 00:06:35,678
Linear voltage extrapolation has over one hundred percent error because load changes make the voltage slope misleading.

78
00:06:35,678 --> 00:06:38,955
These are the kinds of numbers that separate a model from a curve.

79
00:06:39,818 --> 00:06:44,051
For anomaly detection, thresholds are fixed only from normal data.

80
00:06:44,051 --> 00:06:47,899
On one hour of normal operation there are zero false alarms.

81
00:06:47,899 --> 00:06:53,578
A fifty percent resistance step is detected in sixty-three seconds by the residual regression.

82
00:06:53,578 --> 00:07:02,680
A cooling failure under load is detected in three hundred fifty-two seconds, compared with one thousand one hundred fifty-eight seconds for a fixed temperature threshold.

83
00:07:02,680 --> 00:07:07,777
A half-volt voltage-sensor step is caught in one second by the acute residual check.

84
00:07:07,777 --> 00:07:12,534
For robustness, we add five percent packet loss and double the sensor noise.

85
00:07:12,534 --> 00:07:18,942
State-of-charge RMSE grows from zero point zero nine seven to zero point one zero eight percent.

86
00:07:18,942 --> 00:07:24,998
Voltage residual RMS doubles, as expected, and temperature accuracy is unchanged.

87
00:07:24,998 --> 00:07:28,348
This is not perfection, but it is measured degradation.

88
00:07:29,242 --> 00:07:31,109
Now the live integration demo.

89
00:07:31,109 --> 00:07:36,679
The broker, ingestor, twin, simulator, and dashboard are started with one command.

90
00:07:36,679 --> 00:07:40,308
The dashboard is receiving the actual MQTT stream.

91
00:07:40,308 --> 00:07:48,937
We will change the load, inject a cooling fault, read the alarm, run a what-if scenario, and finally trigger the last will by killing the simulator.

92
00:07:49,542 --> 00:07:51,215
This is the live dashboard.

93
00:07:51,215 --> 00:07:54,225
The green banner shows fresh MQTT data.

94
00:07:54,225 --> 00:08:01,033
The top row shows state of charge, state of health from resistance, estimated core temperature, and remaining runtime.

95
00:08:01,033 --> 00:08:06,870
The charts compare measured and twin-predicted voltage, temperature, SoC, and residuals.

96
00:08:06,870 --> 00:08:10,110
The twin is updating on every telemetry sample.

97
00:08:11,042 --> 00:08:14,329
I will apply a constant eighteen-amp load for the next hour.

98
00:08:14,329 --> 00:08:17,666
The dashboard publishes a load command to the simulator.

99
00:08:17,666 --> 00:08:27,157
The plant changes its current; new telemetry arrives at one hertz; and the twin recomputes SoC, power loss, temperature, and remaining runtime.

100
00:08:27,157 --> 00:08:32,763
This is the core digital-twin loop: new data changes the digital representation.

101
00:08:34,417 --> 00:08:37,813
Now I inject a cooling failure while the pack is under load.

102
00:08:37,813 --> 00:08:42,146
The plant reduces its surface-to-ambient conductance to five percent.

103
00:08:42,146 --> 00:08:48,869
The measured temperature rises faster than the twin thermal model expects, so the pre-fit thermal residual grows.

104
00:08:48,869 --> 00:08:54,670
After the residual stays above the calibrated threshold, the detector raises a thermal anomaly.

105
00:08:57,292 --> 00:08:59,183
The alarm is not just a red light.

106
00:08:59,183 --> 00:09:07,072
It shows the thermal residual and the estimated resistance ratio, and it recommends reducing load and inspecting the cooling path.

107
00:09:07,072 --> 00:09:10,809
I follow the recommendation and reduce the load to five amps.

108
00:09:10,809 --> 00:09:15,360
Normal operation produced zero false alarms with the same thresholds.

109
00:09:16,417 --> 00:09:18,150
Next, a what-if decision.

110
00:09:18,150 --> 00:09:22,058
Twenty amps, thirty minutes, thirty-five degree ambient.

111
00:09:22,058 --> 00:09:27,981
The twin starts from the current SoC, resistance, and temperature, and simulates forward in time.

112
00:09:27,981 --> 00:09:34,316
It reports both the time to cutoff voltage and the time to the temperature limit, then recommends a safer current.

113
00:09:34,316 --> 00:09:36,876
The response comes back over MQTT.

114
00:09:38,417 --> 00:09:46,886
The data tab shows the raw and clean SQLite tables, the latest measurements, source versus ingest timestamps, and CSV export.

115
00:09:46,886 --> 00:09:51,133
Every command and what-if request is also logged for traceability.

116
00:09:52,292 --> 00:09:54,317
Finally, I kill the simulator.

117
00:09:54,317 --> 00:09:58,055
The broker publishes the retained last-will offline status.

118
00:09:58,055 --> 00:10:03,504
The dashboard first warns that data is stale, then turns red after the offline threshold.

119
00:10:03,504 --> 00:10:06,635
The twin and historical database remain available.

120
00:10:06,635 --> 00:10:09,013
This is the complete integration path.

121
00:10:14,167 --> 00:10:15,888
What does this project not claim?

122
00:10:15,888 --> 00:10:18,388
It is a simulation, not a real pack.

123
00:10:18,388 --> 00:10:24,007
Cells are treated as identical, so cell imbalance and local hot spots are out of scope.

124
00:10:24,007 --> 00:10:28,206
The one-RC model degrades at low state of charge and low temperature.

125
00:10:28,206 --> 00:10:32,102
The anomaly thresholds are calibrated on one normal duty cycle.

126
00:10:32,102 --> 00:10:39,311
Small sensor offsets are hard to separate from SoC changes, and SQLite is fine for one asset, not a fleet.

127
00:10:39,311 --> 00:10:48,316
Still, the twin closes the loop: measurements update hidden states, alarms explain themselves, and what-if requests use the same physics.

128
00:10:48,316 --> 00:10:53,352
The result is a working, reproducible course project with executable evidence.

129
00:10:53,352 --> 00:10:54,092
Thank you.
